      "q": "1. How will you describe the position of a table lamp on your study table to another person?",
      "a": "Let us consider the given below figure of a study stable, on which a study lamp is placed.\nFrom the above figure,\nLet us consider the lamp on the table as a point\nLet us consider the table as a plane.\nSo we can conclude that the table is rectangular in shape, when observed from the top.\nThe table consists of a short edge and a long edge.\nLet us measure the distance of the lamp from the shorter edge and the longer edge.\nLet us assume\nDistance of the lamp from the shorter edge is 15 cm\nDistance of the lamp from the longer edge is 25 cm.\nTherefore, we can conclude that the position of the lamp on the table can be described in two ways depending on the order of the axes as $(15,{\\text{ 25)}}$ or $(25,{\\text{ 15)}}$.\n2. (Street Plan): A city has two main roads which cross each other at the center of the city. These two roads are along the North\u2013South direction and East\u2013West direction. All the other streets of the city run parallel to these roads and are 200 m apart. There are 5 streets in each direction. Using $1{\\text{ cm\u00a0 =\u00a0 200 m}}$, draw a model of the city on your notebook. Represent the roads/streets by single lines. There are many cross- streets in your model. A particular cross street is made by two streets, one running in the North\u2013South direction and another in the East\u2013 West direction. Each cross street is referred to in the following manner: If the 2nd street running in the North\u2013South direction and 5th in the East\u2013West direction meet at some crossing, then we will call this cross-street $(2,{\\text{ 5)}}$. Using this convention,\nFind:\n(i) How many cross - streets can be referred to as $(4,{\\text{ 3)}}$.\nAns:\nDraw two perpendicular lines as the two main roads of the city that cross each other at the center.\nMark it as N\u2013S and E\u2013W.\nAs per given, take scale as $1{\\text{ cm\u00a0 =\u00a0 200 m}}$.\nDraw five streets that are parallel to both the main roads, to obtain the figure given below:\nStreet plan is as given in the above figure:\n$\\therefore $ There is only one cross street, which can be referred to as $(4,{\\text{ 3)}}$.\n(ii) How many cross - streets can be referred to as $(3,{\\text{ 4)}}$.\nAns:\nThere is only one cross street, which can be referred to as $(3,{\\text{ 4)}}$ .\nConclusion\nThe \"NCERT Solutions for Class 9 Maths Chapter 3: Coordinate Geometry - Exercise 3.1\" provided by Vedantu are crucial for understanding fundamental concepts in coordinate geometry. It's important to focus on mastering the Cartesian coordinate system, plotting points, understanding quadrants, and calculating distances between points. Students should practice these exercises thoroughly to build a strong foundation in geometry, which is essential for higher-level mathematical concepts and applications in various fields. Vedantu's solutions offer comprehensive guidance to ensure thorough understanding and proficiency in these topics.\nClass 9 Maths Chapter 3: Exercises Breakdown\nExercise\nNumber of Questions\nExercise 3.2\n2 Questions & Solutions\nCBSE Class 9 Maths Chapter 3 Other Study Materials\nS. No\nImportant Links for Chapter 3 Coordinate Geometry\n1\nClass 9 Coordinate Geometry Important Questions\n2\nClass 9 Coordinate Geometry Revision Notes\n3\nClass 9 Coordinate Geometry Important Formulas\n4\nClass 9 Coordinate Geometry NCERT Exemplar Solution\n5\nClass 9 Coordinate Geometry RD Sharma Solutions\n6\nClass 9 Coordinate Geometry RS Aggarwal Solutions\nChapter-Specific NCERT Solutions for Class 9 Maths\nGiven below are the chapter-wise NCERT Solutions for Class 9 Maths. Go through these chapter-wise solutions to be thoroughly familiar with the concepts\nS.No.\nNCERT Solutions Class 9 Maths Chapter-wise List\n1\nChapter 1 - Number Systems\n2\nChapter 2 - Polynomials\n3\nChapter 3 - Coordinate Geometry\n4\nChapter 4 - Linear Equations in Two Variables\n5\nChapter 5 - Introduction to Euclids Geometry\n6\nChapter 6 - Lines and Angles\n7\nChapter 7 - Triangles\n8\nChapter 8 - Quadrilaterals\n9\nChapter 9 - Circles\n10\nChapter 10 - Heron's Formula\n11\nChapter 11 - Surface Areas and Volumes\n12\nChapter 12 - Statistics\nImportant Study Materials for CBSE Class 9 Maths\nS.No.\nImportant Related Links for CBSE Class 9 Maths\n1\nImportant Questions for CBSE Class 9 Math\n2\nRS Aggarwal Class 9 Solutions for Math book\n3\nNCERT Class 9 Math Formulas\n4\nNCERT Class 9 Maths Revision Notes\n5\nNCERT Class 9 Maths Exemplar Solutions\n6\nRD Sharma Solutions for NCERT Class 9"
